RTF fixtures for !Letters Batch G2 (RTF open and save)
=======================================================

Small real RTF files (each under 20 KB), the shapes the reader and
the census test (tests/moreapps/rtfcensus.test.mjs) are checked
against. Every file is untouched output of the macOS command
`textutil -convert rtf` (Cocoa's RTF writer, cocoartf2907), from the
source named below, untouched, except the files marked
SPEC-DERIVED at the end: those were written by hand, strictly from
the shapes the RTF 1.9.1 specification gives, for constructs
textutil never writes. They are no evidence of what real Word
writes; replace them with Word-saved files when Word can be driven.

Real Microsoft Word's own RTF dialect is NOT here: Word could not be
driven to Save As RTF without a file-access prompt for a new file
(see the batch notes), so Word-only shapes are missing and must be
added the day Word can write them: footnotes and endnotes, headers
and footers, comments (\*\atrfstart ...), revisions (\*\revtbl,
\revised), sections (\sect, \sectd), Word's \pict pictures and its
list/field/bookmark dialect. textutil does not write any of those
from a .docx (it drops them).

  01-plain-text.rtf            from a .txt file (a tab, two lines)
  02-formatting-docx.rtf       from a hand-built .docx: bold, italic,
                               underline, strike, sizes, super and
                               subscript, centred and right
                               paragraphs, spacing, indents, tab
                               stops, a line break and \page
  03-fonts-colours-html.rtf    from HTML: Arial, Times, Courier,
                               Georgia, colours, a background, a
                               bordered shaded paragraph, \fsmilli
  04-fonts-colours-docx.rtf    from a .docx: fonts and colours (it
                               drops highlight, shading, borders)
  05-lists-html.rtf            from HTML: bullet and numbered lists
                               in three levels, a list starting at E
                               (\listtable, \listoverridetable)
  06-lists-docx.rtf            from a .docx with numbering.xml (it
                               makes every list a bullet list)
  07-table-merges-html.rtf     from HTML: a table with a horizontal
                               merge (\clmgf, \clmrg), a vertical
                               one (\clvmgf, \clvmrg), cell shading,
                               a nested table (\itap2, \nestcell)
  08-table-docx.rtf            from a .docx: a table with merges
                               and a nested table
  09-hyperlinks-html.rtf       from HTML: a HYPERLINK field
  10-picture-attachment.rtf    from an .rtfd made from HTML with an
                               image: Cocoa writes the picture as a
                               {\NeXTGraphic ...} attachment, with no
                               picture data and one raw high byte
  11-non-ascii-docx.rtf        from a .docx: \'hh in cp1252, \uN
                               with \uc0, surrogate pairs as two
                               \u values, escaped \\ { }
  12-styles-html.rtf           from HTML: headings, a block quote and
                               preformatted text (formatting, no
                               \stylesheet)
  13-letter-docx.rtf           from a .docx: a short letter with a
                               small table
  14-sections-docx.rtf         from a .docx with three sections:
                               only the first section's page size
                               and margins survive

SPEC-DERIVED (hand-written from the RTF 1.9.1 specification, not from
any program's output):

  15-spec-word-lists.rtf       Word's list dialect: a \listtable
                               with a three-level numbered list
                               (\leveltext \'02\'00. placeholders,
                               \levelnumbers, \levelfollow, \leveljc)
                               and a \listsimple Symbol bullet
                               (\u-3913), a \listoverride with an
                               \lfolevel start-at override, \ls and
                               \ilvl paragraphs with \listtext, and
                               old-style {\*\pn ...} paragraphs
  16-spec-word-sections.rtf    \facingp, \titlepg, \headerf
                               \headerr \headerl \footer groups, PAGE
                               and NUMPAGES fields in the footer,
                               {\*\bkmkstart}/{\*\bkmkend}, HYPERLINK
                               fields to a bookmark (\\l) and a URL,
                               a locked DATE field, PAGEREF, and three
                               sections (\sect \sectd \sbkpage
                               \lndscpsxn \pgwsxn \cols \colsx
                               \linebetcol \pgnrestart \sbknone)
  17-spec-word-pictures.rtf    Word's pictures: a PNG in hex inside
                               {\*\shppict} with its {\*\picprop}
                               and {\*\blipuid}, a WMF fallback in
                               {\nonshppict}, a JPEG as \bin data
                               scaled 50%, an EMF in hex (\picw
                               \pich \picwgoal \pichgoal). The image
                               data is made by
                               tests/moreapps/pic-fixtures.mjs
                               (pngBytes(4, 2), jpegBytes(1, 1)); the
                               EMF and WMF are bare headers and an
                               end record
  18-spec-word-tables.rtf      Word's table dialect: row definitions
                               before the cells and again in a
                               {\trowd ... \row} group after them,
                               \trhdr \trqc \trrh- \trbrdr* (all six
                               sides), \clvmgf / \clvmrg, \clmgf /
                               \clmrg, \clcbpat \clcfpat \clshdng,
                               \clvertal*, and a nested table with
                               its {\*\nesttableprops ... \nestrow}
                               and {\nonesttables}
  19-spec-word-review.rtf      footnotes, comments, tracked changes:
                               {\footnote} after a {\super \chftn}
                               and one with \ftnalt (an endnote),
                               \fet2 and the note settings (\ftnbj
                               \ftnnar \aenddoc \aftnnrlc...); two
                               comments ({\*\atrfstart}/{\*\atrfend}
                               around text, {\*\atnid} {\*\atnauthor}
                               \chatn {\*\annotation {\*\atnref}
                               {\*\atndate}}), one across runs, one
                               across paragraphs, and a reply naming
                               its parent's \atnref tag in
                               {\*\atnparent} (the spec gives the
                               word, not the form of its value: read
                               as that tag); {\*\revtbl} with two
                               authors, \revised\revauthN\revdttmN
                               and \deleted\revauthdelN\revdttmdelN
                               (DTTM dates), a formatting revision
                               (\crauth \crdate)

Regenerate (macOS): build the source, then
  textutil -convert rtf <source> -output <name>.rtf
